-\\frac{1}{5},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $6$th term is ${a}_{6}=-\\frac{1}{5},$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{6-1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{5} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & =-{a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{5} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & =-{a}_{1}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{5}^{5}}) \\\\ {5}^{4} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ 625 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=625,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =625\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549942 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549942 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549942 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence if the $5$th term is $16$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549942 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1bbae00a-a76f-5b1d-a767-0acf7498bde8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549942 #edb/ref 1074 "The common ratio is $r=\\frac{2}{3},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $5$th term is ${a}_{5}=16,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-1} \\\\ 16 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4} \\\\ {2}^{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (\\frac{{2}^{4}}{{3}^{4}}) \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{4}\\cdot {3}^{4}}{{2}^{4}} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {3}^{4} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ 81 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=81,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =81\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549981 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549981 #edb/ref 1069 "q-265737" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549981 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549981 #edb/ref 1071 "The first term of a geometric sequence is $-\\frac{1}{81},$ and the common ratio is $-3.$ The $9$th term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549981 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91775cc6-b3ff-5403-8a6d-5a5294e1af4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549981 #edb/ref 1074 "The first term is ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{81},$ and the common ratio is $r=-3,$ so we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot (-3)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $9$th term, we can use this formula with $n=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot (-3)^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{9} & =-\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot (-3)^{9-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot (-3)^{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}}\\cdot {3}^{8} \\\\  & =-{3}^{4} \\\\  & =-81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is ${a}_{9}=-81.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549992 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549992 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549992 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549992 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the $5$th term of a geometric sequence is $-125$, and the common ratio is $5,$ find the formula for the $n$th term." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549992 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5f78cff-3c2d-5ff5-b715-c8fb25e53460" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549992 #edb/ref 1074 "The common ratio is $r=5,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $5$th term is ${a}_{5}=-125,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{5-1} \\\\ -125 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{4} \\\\ -{5}^{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{5}^{n-1}}{5} \\\\  & =-{5}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549999 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549999 #edb/ref 1069 "q-265843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549999 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549999 #edb/ref 1071 "If the sixth term of a geometric sequence is $\\frac{1}{16}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{4},$ then the third term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549999 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb4d13f6-05f0-58f1-8216-e5e0e82521cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549999 #edb/ref 1074 "The common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{4},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $6$th term is ${a}_{6}=16,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{6-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{4}^{2}} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{5}} \\\\ \\frac{{4}^{5}}{{4}^{2}} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=64,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{2}} \\\\  & ={4}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third term  is ${a}_{3}=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550015 #edb/ref 1069 "q-265738" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550015 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550015 #edb/ref 1071 "The first term of a geometric sequence is $32,$ and the common ratio is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$  The $12$th term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550015 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f438fee9-fd2e-5bc4-8ed6-aceb6e4fcc33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550015 #edb/ref 1074 "The first term is ${a}_{1}=32,$ and the common ratio is $r=-\\frac{1}{2},$ so we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $12$th term, we can use this formula with $n=12:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =32\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{12} & =32\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{12-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{11} \\\\  & =-{2}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{2}^{11}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{2}^{6}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is ${a}_{12}=-\\frac{1}{64}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
